# Комора — production environment for the demo host (task 59).
#
#   cp .env.prod.example .env.prod    # on the SERVER, then fill in the real values
#   chmod 600 .env.prod
#
# .env.prod is gitignored. Nothing in this file is a real secret; every line says where to get one.
# docker-compose.prod.yml reads it via --env-file, and scripts/deploy.sh passes that flag for you.
#
# This is NOT .env.example. That one is the local-development file, where almost everything may be blank
# and the app still runs. Here the six variables marked REQUIRED are enforced by compose itself: leave one
# empty and `docker compose up` refuses to start with a message naming it, rather than booting a half-dead
# app that fails on the first real message.


# ============================================================================
# REQUIRED — compose refuses to start without these six
# ============================================================================

# --- The public domain (REQUIRED) ---
# The A record you pointed at this server's IPv4 address. No scheme, no trailing slash.
# Everything else that needs a public URL is derived from it in docker-compose.prod.yml:
# the Telegram webhook, the WebApp base URL, and both OAuth callbacks.
# Check it resolves to this box BEFORE the first `up`: a Let's Encrypt failure is rate-limited.
#   dig +short komora.example.com
DOMAIN=komora.example.com

# --- Database (REQUIRED) ---
# Invented here, used nowhere else — this Postgres is not reachable from outside the compose network.
# Generate one rather than typing a word:  openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# Defaults are fine; only change these before the FIRST start, since the volume is initialised once.
POSTGRES_DB=komora
POSTGRES_USER=komora

# --- Telegram (REQUIRED) ---
# From @BotFather. Use the PRODUCTION bot's token, not the development bot's: the two share no state, and
# whichever bot last called setWebhook is the one Telegram delivers to. Two bots pointed at one URL is the
# single most common way a demo bot goes quiet.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# Telegram echoes this back in the X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token header and the app rejects any request
# without it — the only thing standing between a public webhook URL and anyone forging updates. Generate:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Silpo token encryption (REQUIRED) ---
# Base64 of 32 random bytes; encrypts every household's Silpo OAuth tokens at rest.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Leaving it blank generates an ephemeral key: the app boots, but every restart silently logs every
# household out of Silpo and the demo asks the jury to re-authenticate. Set it, and never rotate it
# during the demo window — a changed key makes existing stored tokens undecryptable.
SILPO_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Anthropic Claude (REQUIRED) ---
# https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys — a workspace key, not an identity-linked one, if possible.
# The whole agent is unusable without it: no meal plan, no intent routing, no cart.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=


# ============================================================================
# STRONGLY RECOMMENDED
# ============================================================================

# --- Silpo MCP client id ---
# The OAuth client id issued by Dynamic Client Registration. Blank means the app registers itself on first
# use and logs the id it received.
#
# IMPORTANT: the client id committed in .env.example (AOJkhCwfQQJ5CVTl) is registered for
# http://localhost:8080/auth/silpo/callback and will NOT accept https://$DOMAIN/auth/silpo/callback.
# Leave this BLANK on the server so the app registers a fresh client against the real redirect URI, then
# copy the id out of the log into this file so a redeploy reuses it instead of registering again:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml logs app | grep -i "registered.*client"
SILPO_MCP_CLIENT_ID=

# --- Pitch metrics (task 37) ---
# Shared secret for GET /internal/metrics/pitch and /internal/promotions/report (header X-Metrics-Token).
# That path IS reachable through Caddy, so a blank value is not "unprotected" — blank disables the
# endpoint entirely (404). Set it to read the pitch numbers off the live host:
#   openssl rand -hex 16
METRICS_TOKEN=

# --- The bot's @username ---
# Without the @. Blank costs one getMe lookup the first time a group message needs it, so it is optional —
# but a group round (task 68) recognises «@bot збери напої…» by this name, so setting it removes a
# round-trip from the flow most likely to be demoed live.
TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=


# ============================================================================
# OPTIONAL — each one blank disables its feature cleanly, nothing else breaks
# ============================================================================

# --- Speech to text: voice check-ins ---
# Any OpenAI-compatible /v1/audio/transcriptions endpoint (OpenAI, Groq). Blank and the bot answers a voice
# note by asking for text; every other flow is unaffected.
STT_API_KEY=

# --- Respeecher: spoken replies (text-to-speech, separate from STT above) ---
# Space API key from https://space.respeecher.com. Blank disables spoken replies deployment-wide.
# Even when set, replies stay written per chat until someone sends /voice.
RESPEECHER_API_KEY=

# --- Google Calendar (task 18) ---
# A confirmed delivery slot becomes a calendar event for users who ran /calendar. Blank and /calendar says
# the feature is off; ordering is unaffected.
# Create a Web application OAuth client at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials and add
#   https://<your DOMAIN>/auth/google/callback
# as an authorized redirect URI — the localhost one from development will be rejected here.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Grafana Cloud metrics (task 54) ---
# Only used by the `observability` profile:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod --profile observability up -d alloy
# Alloy scrapes the app's management port inside the compose network and pushes outbound, so no inbound
# port is opened for this. All three blank just means Alloy has nowhere to push.
#
# grafana.com -> your stack -> Prometheus -> "Send Metrics":
#   URL   ends in /api/prom/push
#   USER  is the numeric Prometheus instance id next to it
#   TOKEN is an access-policy token with the metrics:write scope
GRAFANA_CLOUD_PROM_URL=
GRAFANA_CLOUD_PROM_USER=
GRAFANA_CLOUD_PROM_TOKEN=

# --- Anthropic workspace id ---
# Only for an identity-linked key, which is rejected with "anthropic-workspace-id is required".
# An ordinary workspace key needs nothing here.
ANTHROPIC_WORKSPACE_ID=


# ============================================================================
# TUNING — sensible for a deployed demo, change only with a reason
# ============================================================================

APP_NAME=silpo-restock-ai

# Labels every metric series with where it came from, so a laptop rehearsal and this host do not average
# together on the same Grafana dashboard. Leave as `prod` here.
KOMORA_ENV=prod

# How long after the last contact a household is asked what is left. 3d is the real product behaviour;
# shorten it (e.g. 10m) only while rehearsing the check-in flow, and put it back before the jury sees it.
CHECKIN_INTERVAL=3d

# Host-side loopback port for `curl localhost:<port>` while SSH'd in. Not public — docker-compose.prod.yml
# binds it to 127.0.0.1. Change it only if something else on the box already owns 8080.
APP_LOCAL_PORT=8080

# --- Continuous deployment (task 69) ---
# The image Watchtower follows and deploy.sh pulls. Blank uses the default in docker-compose.prod.yml,
# which is what CI publishes from main — the normal flow needs nothing set here.
#
# Set it to pin a specific build. Two reasons you would:
#   - roll back a bad deploy:  APP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/javaandscriptdeveloper/silporestockai:sha-1a2b3c4
#   - freeze the box while the jury is watching, so a push cannot change it mid-demo.
# A pinned tag never moves, so Watchtower has nothing to update and effectively stops deploying.
APP_IMAGE=

# Seconds between checks of ghcr.io. 60 puts a green build on the server in roughly two minutes: CI has to
# build and push first. Raising it costs only patience — `make prod-pull` deploys immediately regardless.
WATCHTOWER_POLL_INTERVAL=60
# No registry credentials appear here because the GHCR package is public, and the image holds no secrets:
# every value in this file is injected at runtime, none of it is baked in. If the package is ever made
# private, Watchtower needs REPO_USER plus a REPO_PASS token with the read:packages scope and nothing
# wider — never a token that can also write packages or read the repository.
